@@ -68,2 +68,21 @@ Bytes 5-7 of the P25 conventional parameter block ([p25-codeplug.md](p25-codeplu
 
+### 2.5 Legacy DES-OFB (`CONFIG_P25_DES`)
+
+A conventional P25 Phase 1 memory channel in the CPS can use single DES-OFB and an eight-octet traffic key of
+algorithm `0x81`. This is for interoperability with legacy systems. NIST withdrew single DES because its
+56 effective key bits provide insufficient protection for modern use. The IV is the first eight octets of
+the message indicator. Discard the first eight keystream octets, then use the same voice and low-speed-data
+intervals as the AES layout above. The two LDUs start their first voice frames at keystream octets 19 and 120
+(zero-based). The HDU and LDU2 carry the selected algorithm and KID, and the LDU1 marks the call encrypted.
+
+The channel's parameter block sets both bits 0 and 1 in byte 5. Firmware that understands the selector refuses
+PTT if its DES-OFB feature or the exact typed key is unavailable. The legacy encryption bit remains set for
+older readers that implement the AES guard. Use current firmware and CPS for DES channels, since older
+versions interpret that bit as AES-256 and cannot read the DES selection. AES and clear channel encodings are
+unchanged. Changing the NAC or destination on the radio preserves the CPS selection.
+
+Host vectors compare voice frames before and after the low-speed-data intervals in both LDUs against OpenSSL
+DES-OFB. Loopback tests exercise encrypted RX and TX across superframes, matching key IDs and disabled builds.
+Over-the-air interoperability with a second radio is unverified.
+
 ## 3. NXDN (`CODEPLUG_KIND_NXDN`)
